{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01Nwayo5XoCPSkeywXGaqujU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JZ2LmjTq3ESv6a4S57epqr","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"grep -r \"inference_cost\\|efficiency\\|token\" /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/artifacts/_project/paper --include=\"*.tex\" 2>/dev/null | head -20"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":703,"cache_read_input_tokens":54211,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":703,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011SjQpRy8YcD8739bUcydpR","session_id":"23fb2834-2812-4457-b569-54861da1d013","uuid":"a3578e63-6175-40bc-bc09-b039eaf609d5"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JZ2LmjTq3ESv6a4S57epqr","type":"tool_result","content":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/artifacts/_project/paper/experiments_3378f2.tex:Instead, the generation formulation appears to have a ceiling imposed by the exact-match evaluation criterion: additional capacity helps the model produce better token-level predictions, but exact-match accuracy is dominated by the hardest output tokens (precision-sensitive numbers, formatting characters) where even small errors cause failure.\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/artifacts/_project/paper/experiments_3378f2.tex:The gap is driven by CWM's catastrophic failure on repo-level code: 53.7--67.2\\% of CWM predictions are unparseable (the model generates execution traces that consume the entire token budget without reaching a verdict).\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/artifacts/_project/paper/experiments_3378f2.tex:\\emph{Token-budget exhaustion} (77\\% of unparseable predictions): the model generates realistic pytest execution traces---dots, warnings, file paths---that consume the entire 1,024-token generation budget before reaching any pass/fail verdict.\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/artifacts/_project/paper/experiments_3378f2.tex:CLS and CWM use different maximum sequence lengths at repo-level (CLS: 8,192 tokens; CWM: 2,048 tokens), raising the concern that CLS's advantage could be an artifact of additional input context rather than formulation.\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/artifacts/_project/paper/introduction.tex:On one end, trajectory-level reward models such as SWE-RM~\\citep{shum2025swerm} score candidate patches as a whole, achieving strong binary accept/reject accuracy but discarding information about \\emph{which} tests a patch fails. This coarse signal limits downstream applications: when a candidate is rejected, the repair system has no guidance for targeted re-repair. On the other end, Code World Models (CWM)~\\citep{meta2025cwm,maimon2026selfexecution} predict execution outputs per test, preserving diagnostic granularity---but formulate the task as open-ended generation, requiring the model to reproduce exact output strings token by token.\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/artifacts/_project/paper/introduction.tex:This generation formulation creates an inherent fragility. When applied to repository-level code with long execution traces, complex return types, and verbose output formatting, CWM predictions encounter two systematic failure modes: \\emph{token-budget exhaustion}, where the expected output exceeds the generation budget and exact-match necessarily fails, and \\emph{string-value prediction errors}, where a single character mismatch in a floating-point number, nested data structure, or formatted string invalidates the prediction regardless of semantic correctness. On repository-level benchmarks with long execution traces, the majority of CWM predictions become unparseable, rendering the generation approach effectively unusable for real-world verification.\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/artifacts/_project/paper/introduction.tex:We argue that these failure modes are not inherent to execution simulation---they are artifacts of the generation formulation. Our key insight is that the verification task requires only a binary pass/fail verdict per test, not a reconstruction of the execution output. We reformulate execution simulation as \\emph{per-test binary classification} (CLS): given a code context and a test case, the model predicts a single token---\\textsc{Pass} or \\textsc{Fail}---sidestepping token-budget exhaustion and string-value prediction entirely (Figure~\\ref{fig:reformulation}).\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/artifacts/_project/paper/introduction.tex:This reformulation advantage is \\emph{complexity-dependent}. On function-level benchmarks where execution outputs are short and simple, CWM failure modes rarely trigger, and the two formulations achieve near-equivalent accuracy: CLS attains 88.43$\\pm$0.78\\% and CWM 87.19$\\pm$0.37\\% across five random seeds (gap: 1.24 percentage points). However, as code complexity increases, the advantage amplifies along two dimensions. First, scaling model size from 4B to 8B widens the gap: CLS reaches 89.39\\% versus CWM at 86.96\\% (gap: 2.43pp, three seeds, $p = 0.0066$), suggesting that additional capacity benefits classification but yields diminishing returns for generation. Second, at repository-level complexity on SWE-bench Verified, the gap grows dramatically: CLS achieves 83.63\\% accuracy versus CWM at 58.04\\%, a 25.59 percentage-point advantage at matched 4B model scale and seed. This gap is driven by the generation failure modes identified above: in our controlled 4B experiments (\\S\\ref{sec:method}), 53.7--67.2\\% of CWM predictions on SWE-bench Verified are unparseable due to token-budget exhaustion or string-value errors.\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/artifacts/_project/paper/method.tex:The model produces a \\emph{single token} as output---either \\textsc{Pass} or \\textsc{Fail}---trained with standard cross-entropy loss over the binary label set. At inference, a single forward pass with greedy decoding yields the verdict for each test. Patch-level correctness is determined by conjunction: the patch is predicted correct if and only if $f$ predicts \\textsc{Pass} for all $t_i \\in T$.\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/artifacts/_project/paper/method.tex:This formulation has two structural properties relevant to our comparison: (1) inference cost is exactly one generated token per test, independent of the complexity of the expected execution output; and (2) the model need not reconstruct any execution output---it must only discriminate between passing and failing behavior.\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/artifacts/_project/paper/method.tex:where $\\hat{o}_i$ is the predicted output string and $\\Sigma^*$ denotes the set of all strings over the output vocabulary. The model is trained with standard language modeling loss to generate the expected output token-by-token. A per-test verdict is derived by exact-match comparison: the test is predicted to pass if and only if $\\hat{o}_i = o_i^*$, where $o_i^*$ is the ground-truth expected output.\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/artifacts/_project/paper/method.tex:Inference cost scales with output length: generating the predicted output for test $t_i$ requires $|\\hat{o}_i|$ tokens, subject to a generation budget $B$. When $|\\hat{o}_i| > B$, the output is truncated and exact-match necessarily fails. Patch-level aggregation follows the same conjunction rule as CLS.\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/artifacts/_project/paper/method.tex:When the expected output $o_i^*$ exceeds the generation budget $B$, the CWM prediction is necessarily truncated: $|\\hat{o}_i| \\leq B < |o_i^*|$, causing exact-match to fail regardless of prediction quality. This failure mode is triggered by long execution outputs (e.g., verbose data structures, stack traces, multi-line formatted output) and becomes more frequent as code complexity increases. CLS is immune: it produces exactly one token irrespective of output length.\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/artifacts/_project/paper/introduction_304198.tex:On one end, trajectory-level reward models such as SWE-RM~\\citep{zhang2025swerm} score candidate patches as a whole, achieving strong binary accept/reject accuracy but discarding information about \\emph{which} tests a patch fails. This coarse signal limits downstream applications: when a candidate is rejected, the repair system has no guidance for targeted re-repair. On the other end, Code World Models (CWM)~\\citep{meta2025cwm,ding2025selfexecution} predict execution outputs per test, preserving diagnostic granularity---but formulate the task as open-ended generation, requiring the model to reproduce exact output strings token by token.\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/artifacts/_project/paper/introduction_304198.tex:This generation formulation creates an inherent fragility. When applied to repository-level code with long execution traces, complex return types, and verbose output formatting, CWM predictions encounter two systematic failure modes: \\emph{token-budget exhaustion}, where the expected output exceeds the generation budget and exact-match necessarily fails, and \\emph{string-value prediction errors}, where a single character mismatch in a floating-point number, nested data structure, or formatted string invalidates the prediction regardless of semantic correctness. On SWE-bench Verified instances, we find that 53.7--67.2\\% of CWM predictions are unparseable at the 4B model scale, rendering the generation approach effectively unusable for repository-level verification.\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/artifacts/_project/paper/introduction_304198.tex:We argue that these failure modes are not inherent to execution simulation---they are artifacts of the generation formulation. Our key insight is that the verification task requires only a binary pass/fail verdict per test, not a reconstruction of the execution output. We reformulate execution simulation as \\emph{per-test binary classification} (CLS): given a code context and a test case, the model predicts a single token---\\textsc{Pass} or \\textsc{Fail}---sidestepping token-budget exhaustion and string-value prediction entirely (Figure~\\ref{fig:reformulation}).\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/artifacts/_project/paper/experiments.tex:Instead, the generation formulation appears to have a ceiling imposed by the exact-match evaluation criterion: additional capacity helps the model produce better token-level predictions, but exact-match accuracy is dominated by the hardest output tokens (precision-sensitive numbers, formatting characters) where even small errors cause failure.\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/artifacts/_project/paper/experiments.tex:The gap is driven by CWM's catastrophic failure on repo-level code: 53.7--67.2\\% of CWM predictions are unparseable (the model generates execution traces that consume the entire token budget without reaching a verdict).\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/artifacts/_project/paper/experiments.tex:\\emph{Token-budget exhaustion} (77\\% of unparseable predictions): the model generates realistic pytest execution traces---dots, warnings, file paths---that consume the entire 1,024-token generation budget before reaching any pass/fail verdict.\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/artifacts/_project/paper/experiments.tex:CLS and CWM use different maximum sequence lengths at repo-level (CLS: 8,192 tokens; CWM: 2,048 tokens), raising the concern that CLS's advantage could be an artifact of additional input context rather than formulation.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011SjQpRy8YcD8739bUcydpR","session_id":"23fb2834-2812-4457-b569-54861da1d013","uuid":"e147a1ef-a62c-4b71-92dd-f6c84ffe9e09","timestamp":"2026-05-16T08:02:36.241Z"}
